  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Logan Webb shut down the Cubs over eight scoreless innings, and the Giants never trailed.",
    "what_this_means": "Webb's command was the game's defining force. Seven hits allowed, seven strikeouts, no walks. The kind of performance that turns a tight contest into controlled territory. The offense didn't need to carry the weight. They scored three in the fifth to build a lead Webb wouldn't surrender, then added two more late when the at-bats still mattered. Casey Schmitt went 2-for-3. Jung Hoo Lee went 2-for-4. Nine hits total, two strikeouts. This was an offense that worked within the space Webb created, not fighting back from behind. Caleb Kilian finished it with two strikeouts in a clean ninth. The formula held: starter dominates, lineup converts runners into runs, game stays in hand. This is the kind of win a fragile team needs to repeat. One where everything aligns, nobody panics, and the margin holds. Eight innings of shutout baseball is not a pattern. It is an outlier. The real test is whether this looks like one clean night or the start of Webb carrying them out of this stretch. Good pitching carries good teams. This team proved it can be carried.",
    "what_to_watch": "Grant Holmes takes the ball tomorrow for Atlanta with a 4.05 ERA, and the Giants will need to generate more than they have lately against quality starting pitching to avoid slipping back into old habits.",
